==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|wīt}}, {{IPA|/waɪt/}}
* {{rhymes|aɪt}}
* {{homophones|lang=en|wight}}; {{a|in accents with the [[wine-whine merger]]}} [[white]]

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|ang|wīten||to accuse, reproach, punish, suspect}}, {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|wītan||to look, behold, see, guard, keep, impute or ascribe to, accuse, reproach, blame}}, derived from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|wīte}}, see below.

====Alternative forms====
* [[wyte]]

====Verb====
{{en-verb|wit|ing}}

# {{context|chiefly|Scotland|lang=en}} To [[blame]]; regard as [[guilty]], [[fault]], [[accuse]]
#* '''Late 14th century''', Geoffrey Chaucer, ‘The Wife of Bath's Tale’, ''Canterbury Tales'':
#*: As help me God, I shal þee nevere smyte! / Þat I have doon, it is þyself to '''wyte'''.
# To [[reproach]], [[censure]], [[mulct]]
# To [[observe]], [[keep]], [[guard]], [[preserve]], [[protect]]

===Etymology 2===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|ang|wītan||guilt, blameworthiness, blame, wrongdoing, misdeed, offense, punishment, retribution, fine, bote, customary rent}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|wīte}}, see below.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|obsolete|_|except|_|Scotland|lang=en}} [[blame|Blame]], [[responsibility]], [[guilt]]
#* '''1485''', Sir Thomas Malory, ''Le Morte Darthur'', Book I:
#*: So many lordes and barons of this reame were displeasyd, for her children were so lost, and many put the '''wyte''' on Merlyn more than on Arthur [...].
#* {{quote-book
 |year=1922
 |year_published=
 |edition=
 |editor=
 |author=E. R. Eddison
 |title=The Worm Ouroboros
 |chapter=
 |url=http://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks06/0602051.txt
 |genre=
 |publisher=The Project Gutenberg, Australia
 |isbn=
 |page=
 |passage=Nor I will not suffer mine indignation so to [[witwanton]] with fair justice as persuade me to put the '''wite''' on Witchland.
}}
# [[punishment|Punishment]], [[penalty]], [[fine]], [[bote]], [[mulct]]

===Etymology 3===
From {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|witan}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb|wit|ing}}

# {{context|obsolete|or|poetic|lang=en}} To [[go]], go away, [[depart]], [[perish]], [[vanish]]

